The Firestore/Storage schema contract for Luma is defined in `docs/FIRESTORE_SCHEMA.md` and must be treated as canonical.

- Preserve a flat collection architecture with `users`, `societies`, `memberships`, and `contributions`; do not model memberships as nested society documents.
- Keep canonical document ID strategies stable: `users/{uid}`, `societies/{autoId}`, `memberships/{societyId}_{userId}`, and `contributions/{autoId}`.
- Ensure required fields, value domains, and type contracts remain aligned with `docs/FIRESTORE_SCHEMA.md` (including enums such as membership roles, contribution statuses, and rule intervals).
- Treat `memberships` as the authoritative bridge for "My Societies" and role checks; do not bypass this bridge in schema or access-control logic.
- Keep Firestore query/index changes aligned with MVP access patterns documented in `docs/FIRESTORE_SCHEMA.md`, especially dashboard membership lookup, pending contribution admin queue, and invite-code lookup.
- Restrict write/update permissions so only society admins can perform admin actions (for example, society-level management and contribution status transitions).
- Enforce tenant privacy boundaries so members can only read contribution data scoped to societies where they hold membership.
- Keep POP storage organization and metadata consistent with the documented path contract and reverse-lookup requirement (`societies/{societyId}/pops/{userId}_{timestamp}.jpg` plus `contributionId` metadata).
- When changing schema behavior, update `docs/FIRESTORE_SCHEMA.md`, `firestore.rules`, `firestore.indexes.json`, and `storage.rules` together in the same change.
